/**
 * @page md_docs_2acip-preparation ACIP packet preparation
 * @ingroup module_network
 * @ingroup acip
 *
 * # ACIP packet preparation
 *
 * Sending a packet sounds simple until the same payload has to work over a
 * synchronous socket, an asynchronous queue, WebSockets, and WebRTC. The
 * preparation layer is the small piece that makes those paths agree on what
 * is being sent. It inspects the ACIP header, applies the negotiated security
 * policy, and hands the transport the buffers it should actually transmit.
 *
 * The important rule is ownership. A plaintext packet can keep borrowing the
 * caller's `iovec` storage. An encrypted packet cannot: its ciphertext belongs
 * to the transport's queued send operation and must stay alive until that
 * operation releases it. WebRTC uses the same encrypted ACIP envelope after
 * peer negotiation; DTLS remains transport security, not a replacement for
 * the ACIP policy.
 *
 * ## What preparation does
 *
 * Preparation reads enough of the header to decide whether the packet is
 * eligible for the current connection. It then chooses one of three outcomes:
 *
 * 1. borrow plaintext vectors without copying their payload;
 * 2. seal an encrypted packet into transport-owned storage; or
 * 3. reject the packet before it reaches a queue or socket.
 *
 * The caller does not need to know whether the transport is TCP, WebSocket, or
 * WebRTC. The caller supplies packet fragments and the negotiated policy; the
 * prepared result carries the lifetime and output contract.
 *
 * @code{.c}
 * acip_packet_plan_t plan = {0};
 * asciichat_error_t err = acip_packet_plan(crypto, buffers, buffer_count, &plan);
 * if (err != ASCIICHAT_OK) {
 *   // Nothing was encrypted or queued.
 *   return err;
 * }
 *
 * if (plan.encrypt) {
 *   size_t wire_length = 0;
 *   err = acip_packet_encrypt(crypto, &plan, control_domain,
 *                             ciphertext, ciphertext_capacity,
 *                             &wire_header, &wire_length);
 *   if (err != ASCIICHAT_OK) {
 *     return err;
 *   }
 *   // Submit ciphertext and keep it alive until the async send consumes it.
 * } else {
 *   // Submit the borrowed buffers while they remain immutable and alive.
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * The plan is intentionally a borrowing description, not an owning packet.
 * Synchronous callers may use the original buffers directly. Asynchronous
 * callers must arrange for the queue to retain or copy them before returning;
 * encrypted callers must also keep their ciphertext storage alive until the
 * transport has consumed it.
 *
 * ## Why the header is inspected first
 *
 * The preparation path must not encrypt or allocate blindly. Header validation
 * catches impossible lengths, unsupported packet families, and packets that
 * violate the connection's state before expensive work begins. That is also
 * why a plaintext path still reads the header: policy and bounds are shared by
 * every transport.
 *
 * In the encrypted path, the existing ACIP nonce and authenticated ciphertext
 * are wrapped in the negotiated record format. The transport sees one prepared
 * envelope; application code never assembles a second, transport-specific
 * crypto format.
 *
 * ## Testing the contract
 *
 * The focused unit test covers borrowed vectors, handshake exceptions,
 * contiguous and fragmented ciphertext, decryption, bounds, allocation and
 * crypto failures, synchronous and asynchronous TCP, and queue rejection.
 * Run it from the build directory:
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * cmake --build build --target test-acip-prepare
 * ctest --test-dir build -R test-acip-prepare --output-on-failure
 * @endcode
 *
 * The integration tests then exercise the same contract through real binary
 * WebSocket frames, WebRTC peers, TCP/WS/WSS policy combinations, and browser
 * DataChannels. A successful test means more than “the bytes arrived”: it
 * means the packet was admitted under the same security and ownership rules
 * on both sides.
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * uv run --project tests/ascii_chat_tests --frozen \
 *   python -m pytest tests/python/integration/acip \
 *   --acip-websocket-probe build/bin/test-acip-prepare
 *
 * uv run --project tests/ascii_chat_tests --frozen \
 *   python tests/integration/crypto_policy.py build/bin/ascii-chat
 * @endcode
 *
 * ## Reading the benchmark numbers
 *
 * The benchmark measures preparation, not network throughput. It counts
 * allocations and explicit payload copies around 64 KiB packets, using real
 * encryption where the path requires it. A lower copy count is useful, but it
 * is not permission to trade away ownership correctness or validation.
 *
 * The most meaningful result from the October 2026 Windows/Clang run was that
 * contiguous encrypted asynchronous TCP and WebSocket preparation avoided the
 * old full-payload copy while plaintext vectors stayed borrowed. Results vary
 * by compiler, CPU, queue pressure, and packet shape, so rerun the benchmark
 * when changing the preparation contract.
 *
 * @see @ref acip "ACIP"
 * @see @ref topic_network "Network I/O"
 */
